# Deny by default, at every depth. Nothing is tracked unless named below.
*

# Directories are re-included so git descends into them; their contents stay
# ignored by the rule above until a pattern below names them. A file cannot be
# re-included while its parent directory is excluded, so this line comes before
# every allow.
!*/

# -- Workspace root ---------------------------------------------------------
!/Cargo.toml
!/Cargo.lock
!/justfile
!/rust-toolchain.toml
!/rustfmt.toml
!/AGENTS.md
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes

# Outward-facing package documentation, which AGENTS.md is not: the README is
# what crates.io and npm render, and `clippy::cargo` requires the `readme` and
# `license` metadata that names these. Internal prose stays in AGENTS.md alone.
!/README.md

# -- Community health -------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub serves these from the **default branch**, so while `main` is the v1
# lineage the repository looks complete and this branch has none of them. At the
# swap they would simply vanish: no security policy, no code of conduct, and no
# issue templates for the form people actually file through. Named here so they
# can exist at all -- every one of these paths is refused by the `*` above, which
# is why copying them across from `main` would have failed silently.
!/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
!/SECURITY.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/.github/CODEOWNERS
!/.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md
!/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*.yml
!/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*.md
!/.github/FUNDING.yml
!/SUPPORT.md
# The v9 -> v10 migration guide. At the root rather than in the package, and
# linked from the README by absolute URL: the npm tarball ships only `dist`,
# `src`, `package.json`, `LICENSE` and `README.md`, so a relative link would
# 404 for exactly the reader it is written for.
!/MIGRATING.md

# -- Brand ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The banners on the three READMEs, and the script that draws them. Drawn by
# meo-canvas itself, so they are proof rather than illustration: if rendering
# breaks, the banner breaks. **Not LFS** -- our own encoder is efficient enough
# that an animated banner is a few hundred kilobytes, and README images are
# fetched on every npm and crates.io page view, which is metered bandwidth on
# LFS and free here.
!/tools/brand/**
!/docs/assets/brand/*.webp
!/docs/assets/brand/*.png
!/docs/assets/brand/README.md
!/LICENSE

# -- Crates -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Each crate carries its own README because `clippy::cargo` requires the
# `readme` metadata and crates.io renders it per crate, not per workspace.
!/crates/*/Cargo.toml
!/crates/*/README.md
!/crates/**/*.rs

# What a test opens rather than constructs: a font a measurement needs, an
# image a decode reads. Scoped to `tests/assets` so a stray binary anywhere
# else in a crate stays denied.
!/crates/*/tests/assets/**

# -- TypeScript surface -----------------------------------------------------
!/packages/*/package.json
!/packages/*/tsconfig*.json
!/packages/*/README.md

# `cargo package` can only carry files under the crate's own directory, so a
# root LICENSE reaches no published crate. Each one gets a copy: a crate that
# declares `license = "MIT"` and ships no licence text is making a claim it does
# not carry, and `cargo package --list` said all four did exactly that.
!/crates/*/LICENSE

# The package manifest's `files` list names LICENSE, so the tarball npm
# publishes carries one rather than claiming MIT with nothing to show.
!/packages/*/LICENSE
!/packages/**/*.ts
!/packages/**/*.js
!/packages/**/*.mjs
# `*.ts` does not cover `*.d.mts`, and one of them is what lets a typechecked
# file import a `.mjs` tool. Ignored, a fresh clone fails `just typecheck` on a
# module that is right there.
!/packages/**/*.d.mts

# The JavaScript reference tool is its own package so it can pin the TypeScript
# TypeDoc loads. Everything it needs to install and run is named here, one file
# at a time -- the first commit of this directory shipped the two `.mjs` files
# the rule above already allowed and silently dropped the config, the manifest
# and the baseline, because `git add -A` does not add what this file denies.
!/packages/*/tools/typedoc/package.json
!/packages/*/tools/typedoc/bun.lock
!/packages/*/tools/typedoc/typedoc.json
!/packages/*/tools/typedoc/tsconfig.json
!/packages/*/tools/typedoc/README.md
!/packages/*/tools/typedoc/undocumented-baseline.txt
!/packages/*/tools/private-docs-baseline.txt

# The vendored tree carries the licence of the project it came from. MIT asks
# for the notice to travel with a substantial portion of the code, and the
# whole JavaScript surface of another project is one. Keeping it beside the
# code rather than in the root LICENSE means it stays true if the vendored
# copy is ever dropped.
!/packages/*/vendor/LICENSE

# -- Formatter --------------------------------------------------------------
# The lockfile is tracked because `bun install --frozen-lockfile` reads it, in
# CI and in `ensure-deps`, and refuses to proceed when it and package.json
# disagree. `packageManager` in package.json names the bun version.
!/bun.lock
!/eslint.config.mjs
!/.prettierrc
!/.prettierignore

# The vitest config, and the reason it is called out rather than swept up by a
# pattern: this file *is* the JavaScript floor. It carries the 90% thresholds,
# `all: true`, and the named exclusions for the three generated tables. Absent,
# vitest still finds the tests and `--coverage` still runs -- with no thresholds
# and a denominator of whatever some test happened to import, so the recipe
# passes and measures nothing. It went untracked from the day it was written,
# and every gate agreed the floor was enforced while CI enforced nothing.
!/vitest.config.mts

# -- Golden fixtures --------------------------------------------------------
# Scenes and the images they must produce. The images are the assertion, so
# they are tracked like source: a diff that changes one is a diff that changes
# what the renderer draws. `.mcs` is the persistence format, named for the
# `MCSC` magic that opens it; `.json` is for a case a human writes by hand.
!/fixtures/**/*.mcs
!/fixtures/**/*.json
!/fixtures/**/*.png

# -- Examples ---------------------------------------------------------------
# A real consumer project, installed by link rather than from a registry, so it
# exercises the package boundary — the exports map, the types field, the entry
# point — rather than reaching into `src` the way the tests do.
#
# Only `src` is tracked. What each example writes goes to its own `out/`, which
# is denied below by name rather than by falling through the default: a reader
# who has just seen `fixtures/**/*.png` allowed deserves to see why these are
# not. A fixture image *is* the assertion — a diff that changes one changes what
# the renderer draws. An example's output is checked against the other surface
# by `just example` at the moment it is written, so it asserts nothing once
# written and tracking it would put megabytes of regenerable pictures in every
# clone.
!/examples/*/package.json
!/examples/*/tsconfig*.json
!/examples/**/*.ts
!/examples/**/*.mjs
!/examples/*/bun.lock
!/examples/*/Cargo.toml
!/examples/**/*.rs

# -- CI ---------------------------------------------------------------------
!/.github/workflows/*.yml
!/.github/dependabot.yml

# The build images the Linux addons are compiled in. Named by extension rather
# than by filename because there is one per libc family and the second arrives
# with musl -- a per-file allow would have to be edited to add a target, which
# is the cost this repository is trying not to pay.
!/containers/Dockerfile.*
!/containers/*.sh

# -- Build output and local configuration -----------------------------------
# Re-ignored after the allows so git prunes them instead of walking every entry
# to discover each one is ignored. `!*/` above otherwise makes it descend into
# all of them.
#
# `.cargo/` holds the `[patch.crates-io]` that points meo-skia-canvas at a
# sibling checkout. That patch is one machine's convenience, and committing it
# would break every clone that does not have the sibling.
target/
node_modules/
dist/
examples/*/out/
.cargo/
# What `just pack` writes: two tarballs and the staged platform package, one of
# them a 51 MB binary. A release is built from a commit, never committed.
release/

# Evaluation scratch: agent-owned reports, merged by hand, never committed
.tmp/
